      ":question/id": "029fdb6a-d6ee-5a1f-bdd5-c75825b67c96",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-99635",
      ":question/problem": "$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product” is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(-1)(1)+2(1)(2)+(1)(-3) \\\\  & =(-1)+4-3 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement “${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product” is false since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(2)(1)+2(-1)(2)+(2)(-3) \\\\  & =2-4-6 \\\\  & =-8\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥̸{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement “$S={{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product” is false. Since ${v}_{2}⊥̸{v}_{3},$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product” only."
    }
